SHEET 1 880 892
TEXT 0 72 Left 2 !G1 Rm 0 VALUE={iu-IuN*(a*v(phi)/phiN+b*(v(phi)/phiN)^m)} tripdv=1 tripdt=1\nR1 Rm 0 1e8\nE1 phi 0 VALUE={N*iu/v(Rm)} tripdv=1 tripdt=1
TEXT 0 160 Left 2 !.step param iu 1u 1000 5
TEXT 0 136 Left 2 !.op
TEXT 0 184 Left 2 !Vrmair rm_air 0 {L*0.1/A0/mu0}
TEXT 0 120 Left 2 !Vmur_air mur_air 0 1
TEXT 0 32 Left 2 !.param IuN=0.5 N=1000 iu=1 A0=1 L=4 mu0=4*pi/1e7\n.param PhiN=1 m=7 a=0.3 b={1-a}
TEXT 0 112 Left 2 !Emur mur 0 VALUE={L/A0/mu0/v(Rm)} tripdv=1 tripdt=1
TEXT 144 184 Left 2 ;Length of iron core is ten times length of airgap
TEXT 0 200 Left 2 ;This example shows the effect of high saturation of the \niron core of a transformer. The transformer ironcore length\nis 4m and the airgap length=40cm. At low phi the magnetic\nresistance is mainly determined by the airgap resistance\nwhich is about 300k. Now the current (and flux) gets higher and higher\ndue to the switching on at voltage zero and the additional full \nremanent flux at startup. So the flux is 3 times the nominal flux at the end\nand the magnetic resistance of the core is now about 10 times the value\nof the airgap. Since the core length is ten times the length of the airgap\nthe resistance values of airgap and core are about the same
